{"kind":"assert","text":"Stage 4 decision: a checker service's secondary machine commentary follows the application's declared vocabulary.\n\nSource boundary: at stage-2 code head 57c84188702bf9e1443eeef3ae9ac3ffb14a0be7, packages/checkers/src/checker.ts:246–259 signs a v:1 literal note; packages/checkers/src/llm.ts:123–129 invokes it after the primary check. R-DECL-18 currently selects only the check act. This is the remaining question in builder assert fcbdf07e. These source observations are not an approval or runtime probe.\n\nFor v2, configure any secondary commentary explicitly: select its declared kind and the declared text field the service fills. Do not infer the name note, silently choose the first comment act, or gain authority from the selector. The selected entry target must run only comment, allow checker under who, and have a text field that the service can fill within its declared UTF-8 byte limit, with no other required field left unfilled. Validate that mapping against the same policy/configuration version, and carry the selected kind, exact binding and bounded field mapping in the Room-issued job. Builder owns the smallest concrete type/schema and the corresponding protocol amendment as part of the existing whole stage-4 request. Ordinary checkers need no secondary-comment mapping. The v2 code-review LLM configuration supplies it so its existing anchored, machine-generated, advisory commentary remains available; v1 continues its present note behavior.\n\nSign this secondary intent outside the sandbox, explicitly bound to the retained job descriptor and anchored to the accepted check. Ordinary current declaration, role, grant-map, body, binding and idempotency guards apply. A mapping grants neither membership nor a human review verdict. Keep both the primary check and secondary commentary labelled machine generated; the LLM check remains advisory and volatile, and neither can meet a review obligation. Changes to a configured mapping follow the existing admin-approved configuration path.\n\nOnce the primary check is recorded, refusal or failure of the secondary comment does not undo that result, turn it into review evidence, or rerun the expensive checker/model. Preserve findings in the primary check detail and expose the secondary outcome through the existing diagnostic path. Never silently replace the job's binding to make a refused comment pass. Cover activation between dispatch, primary admission and secondary admission, together with exact secondary retries, missing/stale grants, missing mapping, changed kind and changed text-field names, UTF-8 limits, malformed mapping, and unchanged v1 controls. Add named compiling mutants for the new guards and verify the accepted check survives each secondary failure.\n\nStage 4 retains every existing acceptance condition and the complete R-DECL-18 lifecycle in fcbdf07e/1170698e. Its report and exact-head independent review include this secondary seam and its configuration/protocol/type/test paths. Stage 2 discloses the secondary v2 producer gap honestly; passing a fake ledger's note test is not evidence that a genuine v2 Room accepts that note. This decision does not weaken the stage-2 full code-review suite criterion, approve an implementation, or add full stage 4 as a jam development start gate. Builder still judges readiness for the concrete first jam task.","body":{"implementation":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:48c021ea67470bc59bdf48f8d6a1408cbc06025a","no_git_artifact":"true"}}